          "type": "reasoning",
          "text": "Let me analyze. Requirement: flat grammar, word ends at first closing brace, not recursively interpolated. Grader 'literal' case: A=${BASE:+$OTHER} expects '$OTHER' literal. C=x${BASE:+α}${BASE:+β}y → xαβy. D=${BASE:+unfinished → kept literal.\n\nCan the grader accept a repair violating flat grammar? Consider a repair that treats word ending at first closing brace: ${BASE:+a}b} → 'ab}'. Grader doesn't test that. But could a repair recursively interpolate $OTHER and pass? A=${BASE:+$OTHER} with OTHER='expanded' — recursive expansion would give 'expanded', failing grader. So that's caught.\n\nWhat about nested expansion within word, e.g., ${BASE:+${OTHER}}? Flat grammar: word ends at first closing brace → word is '${OTHER', and rest '}' is literal. Result would be '${OTHER}'? Wait word='${OTHER' substituted → '${OTHER' then trailing '}' literal → '${OTHER}'. A recursive repair would give 'expanded'. Grader doesn't test this. But does that violate \"flat, nonrecursive interpolation\"? The grader's literal test only checks $OTHER (bare dollar), which recursive-on-word would also catch... Actually a repair that recursively interpolates word would expand $OTHER too, failing A test. But a repair that only handles nested braces ${OTHER} but not bare $OTHER would pass grader while violating flat grammar. That's a plausible counterexample: input 'A=${BASE:+${OTHER}}' with OTHER=expanded, expected flat result '${OTHER}' (word='${OTHER' + '}'), wrong repair yields 'expanded}'... hmm wait recursive: word='${OTHER}'? Depends. A repair adding nested brace matching would parse word as '${OTHER}', expand to 'expanded'. Grader never tests braces inside word, so accepted. That violates \"word ends at the first closing brace and is not recursively interpolated.\"\n\nCheck grader cases: C=x${BASE:+α}${BASE:+β}y — no braces in word. D=${BASE:+unfinished — malformed, kept literal. So indeed a nested-brace repair passes all grader cases but violates flat grammar.\n\nConcrete counterexample: BASE=yes, OTHER=expanded, input 'OUT=${BASE:+${OTHER}}'. Requirement (word ends at first closing brace): word is '${OTHER', substituted literally, remainder '}' → '${OTHER}'... wait word '${OTHER' substituted as literal text → result '${OTHER' + '}' = '${OTHER}'. Wrong nested repair yields 'expanded'.\n\nCitations: requirement text and grader literal case.",
