        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "I submitted the review finding.\n\n**Summary of the finding:** The requirement demands a flat expression grammar — the `${NAME:+word}` word ends at the first closing brace and is not recursively interpolated. However, the grader's `literal` case only tests brace-free words (`$OTHER`, empty, `α`/`β`) and a malformed unterminated expression. It never places a `}` inside the word, so a repair that adds nested-brace matching (expanding `${OTHER}` inside the word while leaving bare `$OTHER` literal) passes all grader cases while directly violating the flat-grammar rule. Concrete counterexample: with `BASE=yes, OTHER=expanded`, `OUT=${BASE:+${OTHER}}` must yield `'${OTHER}'` per the requirement, but the nested-expansion repair yields `'expanded'` and is accepted.",
